<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dwight D. Eisenhower</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1890">1890</a>–<a href="/years/1969">1969</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Eisenhower</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dwight D. Eisenhower was a US general and President of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> from 1953 to 1961. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-crisis">Suez Crisis</a> of 1956 he pressed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> to withdraw from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> by threatening to withhold financial support for the pound, a humiliation that exposed the limits of British power and accelerated the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dwight-d-eisenhower.jpg" alt="Dwight D. Eisenhower" width="320" height="418" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: White House · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dwight_D._Eisenhower,_official_photo_portrait,_May_29,_1959_(cropped)(3).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Suez Crisis of 1956 delivered the public humiliation that confirmed what financial reality had already established: when President <b>Eisenhower</b>'s administration refused support for sterling and blocked British access to IMF funds unless Britain withdrew from Egypt, Prime Minister Anthony Eden's climbdown demonstrated that Britain could no longer pursue…</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1957 President <b>Dwight D. Eisenhower</b> sent paratroopers to escort nine Black students into Central High School in Little Rock, Arkansas, after Governor Orval Faubus had used the National Guard to bar them.</blockquote><a href="/movement/civil-rights-movement">Civil rights movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Some 56,000 people had died there; <b>Eisenhower</b> later had local Germans brought to see it.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-11">11 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>President <b>Eisenhower</b> proclaimed Hawaii's admission as the 50th state.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-21">21 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The next day it broadcast a recorded Christmas message from President <b>Eisenhower</b>, the first voice transmitted from space.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-18">18 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>First Lady Mamie <b>Eisenhower</b> christened Nautilus at Groton, Connecticut.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-21">21 January in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Eisenhower Birthplace State Historic Site, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in Walter Reed Army Medical Center, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.75,-96.53&amp;year=1941" aria-label="Eisenhower Birthplace State Historic Site on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2087 940 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Dwight D. Eisenhower"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2537 1210h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2537 1210h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.75,-96.53&amp;year=1941">What was here in 1941? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;IN THE COUNCILS of government, we must guard against the acquisition of unwarranted influence, whether sought or unsought, by the military-industrial complex.&#8221;</p><footer>Farewell Address (1961) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Eisenhower%27s_farewell_address_(reading_copy)" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Only an alert and knowledgeable citizenry can compel the proper meshing of the huge industrial and military machinery of defense with our peaceful methods and goals, so that security and liberty may prosper together.&#8221;</p><footer>Farewell Address (1961) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Eisenhower%27s_farewell_address_(reading_copy)" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Every gun that is made, every warship launched, every rocket fired signifies, in the final sense, a theft from those who hunger and are not fed, those who are cold and are not clothed.&#8221;</p><footer>The Chance for Peace, address to the American Society of Newspaper Editors (1953) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Chance_for_Peace" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 535 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
